{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested for exact head 53066f1a6985535bd184496694741db4dc22a4f0, tree 8b84f3cfbdea533aef3890dabbc3553e9f23fad4. Reporting artifact 7bff542a was FIRST FULL read; all 58 bound artifacts remain in this review. The complete plan 031 scope is preserved.\n\nP2 — Completed preparations with no act permanently consume unresolved capacity (service.ts:641-664; custody.ts:243-265,327-378; scope/core.ts:740-750; derive/fold.ts:226-238). The new route keeps a parent record in prepared and records the step answer, but never submits an act or terminalizes that parent. Native preparations use putPrepared; only acts use putAccepted(actor,key), which native settlement reads. Even definitively accepted or refused standalone steps therefore cannot resolve their parent through settle or become eligible for terminal archival. After 256 distinct completed standalone identities, custody refuses unrelated new work. Give standalone preparations an explicit lifecycle and settlement projection: decisive step outcomes must be retained, replayable and terminal; lost/unavailable outcomes must remain unresolved and retry the exact signed step. Do not represent a preparation as an accepted act. Witness terminal replay and restart, admission after more than 256 completed standalone identities, and continued protection of unknown outcomes.\n\nP2 — Recovering a dead reclaim lock can still break mutual exclusion (node.ts:81-114). A process reads dead reclaim token R0; another replaces it with active RA before the first renames the pathname. The first moves RA away. A third process can create RC before attempted hardlink restoration, leaving the owners of RA and RC both inside the underlying reclaim section. Both can read the underlying dead lock; one can then unlink the other's newly created live lock between read-again and unlink. The primary's disclosure and ordinary-dead-owner witness do not fulfill exclusion when a reclaimer dies. Use a recovery protocol that cannot displace an active reclaimer, or safely refuse that stale-reclaim recovery. Witness the actual replacement/interleaving after death inside reclaim. Preserve process-liveness authority; deadlines are not terminal outcomes.\n\nP2 — Backward outcome lookup retains and repeatedly scans the entire uncertain history (custody.ts:214-230). Each 64-entry slice is prepended to tail and decisive rescans the accumulated array. Repeated Lost/unavailable sends stay in the same attempt, append outcomes and spill without discarding them. A not-found settlement after N such outcomes therefore uses O(N) heap and O(N²/64) copying/scanning despite bounded I/O calls. Search slices independently backwards, stopping at the newest decisive outcome or older-attempt boundary. Retain complete disk history. Witness bounded working storage and correct all-spilled/current-attempt boundaries.\n\nP2 — Standalone names bypass the 4 KiB result bound (service.ts:447-449,630-664,95-99; client/intent.ts:92-104; bytes/records.ts:50,126; derive/prepare.ts:180-192). Capability and step accept arbitrary strings without a length bound. A 100 KiB ASCII capability with a short step and empty fields fits the 2 MiB custody record; native judgment can normally refuse the undeclared name. The result then echoes the complete names in both its sentence and structured step. ok removes duplicated text but retains oversized structuredContent, producing more than 200 KiB. Validate/bound names before signing or bound the projection while retaining the exact envelope. Add a whole-result refusal witness. The single large read-item exception does not cover this prepare result.\n\nP2 — Successful legacy notifications still receive replies (wire.ts:75,152-177; node.ts legacy POST response path). The classifier recognizes a method without id as a notification, but initialize, tools/list and tools/call unconditionally return reply(undefined,...), unlike ping/default. Stdio emits a result with no id; HTTP responds with 200 JSON rather than accepted-notification 202 with no body. This inherited defect remains after the claimed classification repair. JSON-RPC 2 section 4.1 forbids notification replies (https://www.jsonrpc.org/specification#notification); MCP 2025-11-25 Sending Messages requires accepted notifications/responses to return 202 and no body (https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2025-11-25/basic/transports#sending-messages-to-the-server). Suppress successful notification replies, or explicitly reject request-only methods before dispatch. Witness notification silence on both transports and define whether tools/call executes. This is not an authentication bypass: the call uses the same authenticated caller/key.\n\nP3 — Clarify cursor instructions (docs/mcp.md:580 and corresponding descriptor). “A cursor continues only the traversal it came from” suggests enforced binding, but cursor data lacks traversal/type validation. Tell callers to retain the same scope, type and final flag. The live and retained traversals are now correctly documented.\n\nArchitecture: The requested no-act path and accepted instance/stage/check/hand-in story are present, with preparation and act receipts distinct. The full two-file change-demo3/version-3-destination proposal now reaches accepted merge/publication in both modes; the demo source-shape refusal remains a separate case. The Host outside-operation answers are explicitly a stand-in, not repository staging or commit-walking proof. Stable configuration refresh and membership-scoped role descriptions repair the earlier discovery defects. The remaining standalone lifecycle and exclusion findings block reliable general-purpose custody.\n\nSecurity: Refused resubmissions now acquire the shared capacity lease and persist unresolved status before delivery; archive candidates are reread under their locks. Invalid IDs and method/version reflections are bounded, stream closure during pending snapshot prevents orphan subscriptions, and transport authentication/Origin/UTF-8/containment behavior remains inherited. The remaining reclaim race and unbounded no-act response must be repaired. Join's guarantee is now honestly limited to its own result; authorized native membership history still contains the signed invitation secret unchanged. This follows the prior requested qualification and does not mask integrity-bearing records.\n\nSimplification: Preserve the complete capabilities and exact retained history. Explicit standalone lifecycle, independent backward slice search and one consistent notification response rule simplify these repairs without new history eviction, scope cuts or altered native semantics. The existing projection and settlement paging repairs are credited; the twenty-step witness covers complete step-name recovery and whole CallResult bounds.\n\nComposition: Independently audited against receiving main 6198b199e220e6ec2d7ee389937e4e2601f124ab. All 1,416 unrelated receiving leaves are identical; exactly 58 paths differ, every one bound here. The prior unbound Docs/Home/observability composition issue is repaired. No assertion about a future merge replaces this exact-tree check.\n\nCoverage and evidence: Five bounded peer finals and their body/diff/inheritance audits were FULL read by Root. Native Git and Python standard-library data audits checked body/diff hashes, equal spans and complete reconstructions. Primary 25,918 B/361 LF SHA256 ba5a2e2b7f82a07ea63d131b204753773ce8ef231d26463cc74d3d1d542be330 is FIRST FULL Root. Changed authored bodies have complete qualified coverage through prior FULL native-equal spans plus FULL fresh reads; selected supplementary native functions are complete units, not blanket whole-module credit. Node is FULL through the transport reviewer; custody alone claims storage/partial whole-module coverage. Tools JSON has FULL lossless data-token coverage, NOT raw BODY FULL: raw 244,445 B SHA256 5eef5f5e46c3ebb86c58c7ee5a55861e46be323f2cff587efd0f99cbc7f62acb; token project